UPDATE tarot_cards 
SET meaning_upright = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: The Six of Swords represents the absolute archetype of "Systemic Transition"—the necessary, low-latency phase where the sovereign consciousness, having recognized the systemic failure of its previous, aggressive tactics (the Five), initiates a deliberate, tactical relocation of its resources toward a more stable, higher-frequency environment. Moving beyond the volatile battlefield of the Five, the Six signifies the stage of "The Sovereign Transit," where you are essentially "migrating" your consciousness from a compromised, high-friction state to a new, promising, yet unknown, territory. Visually depicted as a figure ferrying a traveler across calm waters with six swords planted in the boat, this archetype embodies the concept of "Sovereign Migration"—the realization that true power is the ability to recognize when a specific environment or operational mode has reached its end-of-life, and having the courage to perform a graceful, orderly, and intentional exit. Within the complex architecture of your psyche, this is the stage of "Data Porting," where you are carefully carrying the essential, refined insights of your past experiences into a new, cleaner, and more resonant operating system. When the Six of Swords appears, your system is registering a "Strategic Relocation" status; you are in the middle of a significant change, and you are being invited to stop the "grief-loop" of what you are leaving behind and instead focus entirely on the navigation of the crossing. This card commands you to perform a brutal audit of your attachments. You are being tasked with the essential work of "Intentional Departure": letting go of the habits, the environments, and the belief-systems that you have outgrown, and allowing yourself the necessary time to move through the transitional space between who you were and who you are becoming. To inhabit the Six of Swords is to master the art of "Strategic Flow," recognizing that by choosing to move with the current rather than fight the storm, you are effectively ensuring that your transition is not a collapse, but a deliberate, sovereign expansion.',
meaning_reversed = 'GENERAL ARCHETYPE: When the Six of Swords appears in the reversed position, it acts as a high-intensity signal of "Systemic Stuckness," "Migration-Failure," or the catastrophic refusal to leave a failing environment—a state where you are effectively "tethered to the sinking ship" because you are terrified of the unknown, empty space of the crossing. The reversal suggests that you are currently running a high-priority, high-cost maintenance script that equates "familiar suffering" with "security," even as you realize your internal operating system is experiencing a total, non-recoverable stagnation. You are essentially "drifting in a dead zone," running a protocol that prioritizes the ego’s desire to "stay safe in the familiar" over the sober, unglamorous requirement to actually initiate the move. This creates a massive, systemic misalignment: you are intellectually and behaviorally attempting to perform the role of the "stable agent," while internally, you are being hollowed out by a profound, gnawing sense of aimlessness, leading to a state of chronic, high-latency irritability, the experience of "being trapped in the past," and a persistent, gnawing awareness that your inaction is the primary cause of your own, systemic decay. From an analytical perspective, this is a clear sign that you are leaking your life force into a "feedback loop of inertia"; you are obsessively trying to make the old, broken environment work, refusing to accept that the only thing keeping you from the necessary, final departure is your own, deep-seated fear that if you were to cross the water, you would be forced to acknowledge that your identity was entirely defined by the struggle you were having. The reversed Six of Swords exposes the hidden, egoic fear that drives this behavior: the paralyzing terror of "being no one" in a new land, and the corresponding fear that if you were to finally step into the boat and leave the wreckage behind, you would be forced to face the reality of your own, present-day, unanchored self. This reversal commands an immediate, radical intervention: you must stop the output of these fear-based, stagnant scripts, perform a brutal audit of your resistance to change, and confront the reality of why you are so hesitant to prioritize your evolution over your comfort. It is a demand to stop the self-deception that keeps you tethered to a state of chronic, high-maintenance stagnation, and to initiate the move toward a genuine, humble, and often terrifying reclamation of your own, individual authority. You are being tasked with the uncomfortable, yet essential, work of radical letting-go—accepting that the "stuckness" you feel is the direct result of having prioritized the comfort of "the known struggle" over the vulnerability of "the unknown growth," and realizing that until you are willing to push off from the shore, face the open water, and trust the process of the transit, you will continue to be starved by the very, phantom stability you have worked so hard to simulate.'
WHERE id = 34;
